const CHARACTERS=[
{id:'arjuna',name:'Arjuna',sa:'अर्जुन',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'The courage to be a learner',role:'Pandava archer; the listener in the Gita.',story:'Arjuna openly admits confusion and asks Krishna for instruction. At the dialogue’s end he responds with a considered resolve to act.',lesson:'Competence and uncertainty can coexist. Asking a clear question can be a form of strength.',practice:'In your next class or meeting, ask the question you were hiding to appear capable.',nuance:'His battlefield duty belongs to the epic’s context; it is not a blanket instruction to fight people in everyday life.',ref:'Bhagavad Gita 2.7; 18.73',gita:['2.7','18.73']},
{id:'krishna',name:'Krishna',sa:'श्रीकृष्ण',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Guidance that invites reflection',role:'Arjuna’s charioteer and teacher in the Gita.',story:'Krishna teaches at length and then asks Arjuna to reflect fully and choose. The dialogue combines devotion, instruction and deliberation.',lesson:'When helping a friend, share your reasoning and leave room for their agency.',practice:'Ask “Would you like me to listen, or help you think through options?” before giving advice.',nuance:'Krishna’s divine role is a matter of the text and Hindu faith, not simply a modern leadership archetype.',ref:'Bhagavad Gita 18.63',gita:['18.63']},
{id:'draupadi',name:'Draupadi',sa:'द्रौपदी',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Question an unjust premise',role:'Queen of the Pandavas; a central moral voice in the dice-hall crisis.',story:'Draupadi asks whether Yudhishthira lost himself before staking her. Her question challenges the authority claimed over her.',lesson:'A confident assertion by someone powerful still deserves scrutiny. Ask what gives a demand its legitimacy.',practice:'When pressured into an unfair demand, calmly ask what rule or agreement supports it.',nuance:'This is an account of injustice, not a recommendation that a harmed person must argue perfectly to deserve protection.',ref:'Sabha Parva, Ganguli §LXVI',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m02/m02066.htm'},
{id:'vidura',name:'Vidura',sa:'विदुर',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Do not hide behind silence',role:'A counsellor in the Kuru court.',story:'During the dice-hall episode, Vidura calls on the assembly to answer Draupadi’s question. The scene puts responsibility on those who witness wrongdoing.',lesson:'Loyalty to an institution should not erase your responsibility to speak about harm.',practice:'If a teammate is being treated unfairly, name the specific issue and seek a fair response.',nuance:'Speaking safely may require allies or an appropriate reporting channel; courage has more than one form.',ref:'Sabha Parva, Ganguli §LXVII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m02/m02067.htm'},
{id:'karna',name:'Karna',sa:'कर्ण',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Talent needs moral discernment',role:'A warrior allied with Duryodhana.',story:'In the dice hall, Karna opposes Vikarna’s defense of Draupadi and supports her humiliation. This troubling conduct must remain visible alongside admiration for his abilities.',lesson:'Ability, loyalty and personal suffering do not make every action right. Judge a choice by its effect on others too.',practice:'Ask whether defending your friend is turning into defending a harmful action.',nuance:'No epic character should be reduced to a single trait, nor should cruelty be excused by a heroic image.',ref:'Sabha Parva, Ganguli §LXVII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m02/m02067.htm'},
{id:'hanuman',name:'Hanuman',sa:'हनुमान्',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Strength expressed through speech',role:'Sugriva’s envoy and Rama’s devoted ally.',story:'When Hanuman first meets Rama and Lakshmana, Rama praises his measured and learned speech. His introduction combines purpose with care.',lesson:'Speak clearly, at a considered pace, and with attention to the listener. Respect can make an introduction more effective.',practice:'Before an important introduction, decide your purpose and say it in three clear sentences.',nuance:'The lesson comes from this episode, not from a claim that confident speech guarantees trust.',ref:'Kishkindha Kanda 4.3, especially 28–35',url:'https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/sloka?field_kanda_tid=4&field_sarga_value=3&language=ro'},
{id:'bharata',name:'Bharata',sa:'भरत',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Stewardship without possession',role:'Rama’s brother, who carries back Rama’s sandals.',story:'In Ayodhya Kanda 112, Bharata receives Rama’s sandals and honors them. The episode expresses his refusal to treat Rama’s place as a personal prize.',lesson:'Holding responsibility for someone else requires accountability, not a sense of ownership.',practice:'When entrusted with a shared resource, leave clear records and make it easy to hand back.',nuance:'The sandals belong to the devotional and royal symbolism of the narrative; the workplace example is a modern analogy.',ref:'Ayodhya Kanda 2.112',url:'https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/sloka?field_kanda_tid=2&field_sarga_value=112&language=dv'},
{id:'rama',name:'Rama',sa:'राम',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Protection and refuge',role:'Prince of Ayodhya; central figure of the Ramayana.',story:'In the discussion about Vibhishana’s arrival, Rama declares his commitment to protect one who seeks refuge. The scene includes counsel and disagreement before his decision.',lesson:'Listen to concerns, then take responsibility for the values you choose to uphold.',practice:'When someone asks for help, listen carefully and offer support within what you can responsibly provide.',nuance:'A modern application should not require ignoring personal safety or placing unlimited trust in everyone.',ref:'Yuddha Kanda 6.18',url:'https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/sloka?field_kanda_tid=6&field_sarga_value=18&language=ro'}
];
CHARACTERS.push(
{id:'yudhishthira',name:'Yudhishthira',sa:'युधिष्ठिर',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Fairness beyond personal advantage',role:'Eldest Pandava; questioned by the Yaksha in the forest.',story:'When the Yaksha offers to revive one brother, Yudhishthira chooses Nakula. He explains that Kunti already has a surviving son in himself, and Madri should also have one. The choice places equal regard for his two mothers above recovering the strongest ally.',lesson:'A fair decision considers who is being overlooked, even when a different choice would benefit you more.',practice:'Before assigning an opportunity in your team, write the criteria and check whether you would use the same criteria for someone outside your closest circle.',nuance:'This is one exemplary decision, not a claim that every choice Yudhishthira makes is just. His conduct in the dice game also requires moral scrutiny.',ref:'Vana Parva, Ganguli §CCCXI · the Yaksha’s questions',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m03/m03311.htm'},
{id:'bhima',name:'Bhima',sa:'भीम',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Strength that can accept correction',role:'Pandava warrior; Hanuman’s brother through their connection to Vayu.',story:'Bhima demands passage from a monkey lying in his way, but cannot move its tail despite his strength. He then apologizes for his harsh words and asks to learn who the monkey is. Hanuman reveals his identity.',lesson:'Confidence becomes more useful when you can recognize its limits. An apology can turn a contest of pride into a chance to learn.',practice:'When a task exposes a gap in your skill, ask someone experienced to show you their method before trying to overpower the problem.',nuance:'The encounter is an epic narrative with a divine figure, not a physical demonstration to reproduce. Its practical lesson here is an editorial reflection on humility.',ref:'Vana Parva, Ganguli §CXLVI',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m03/m03146.htm'},
{id:'bhishma',name:'Bhishma',sa:'भीष्म',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Knowledge must meet responsibility',role:'Elder of the Kuru house; an influential voice in its assembly.',story:'When Draupadi challenges the staking of her in the dice hall, Bhishma says that dharma is subtle and that he cannot resolve her question. Meanwhile, the humiliation continues. His uncertainty is part of the episode’s troubling picture of authority.',lesson:'Recognizing complexity matters, but it must not become an excuse to leave someone without protection. Expertise carries responsibility for what happens while a debate continues.',practice:'If a group is debating a complaint, first ask what immediate step can protect the person affected while the facts are examined.',nuance:'This lesson examines a failure in a particular scene. It neither erases Bhishma’s other qualities nor endorses the patriarchal assumptions voiced in the assembly.',ref:'Sabha Parva, Ganguli §LXVI',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m02/m02066.htm'},
{id:'duryodhana',name:'Duryodhana',sa:'दुर्योधन',epic:'Mahabharata',trait:'Notice when pride blocks accountability',role:'Leading Kaurava prince; resists Krishna’s peace counsel.',story:'During the peace embassy, Duryodhana insists that he sees no fault in himself. He defends what happened in the dice games and refuses to give the Pandavas even a needle-point’s amount of land.',lesson:'Treating every criticism as hostility makes correction almost impossible. Firmness becomes destructive when protecting your image matters more than examining harm.',practice:'In your next disagreement, name one part of the problem you contributed to before explaining the other person’s part.',nuance:'Duryodhana is more than a label for a difficult person. This episode is used to examine a pattern of refusal, not to diagnose or condemn people you disagree with.',ref:'Udyoga Parva, Ganguli §CXXVII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m05/m05127.htm'},
{id:'sita',name:'Sita',sa:'सीता',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Discernment under pressure',role:'Rama’s wife; held captive in Lanka when Hanuman finds her.',story:'Hanuman offers to carry Sita away. She acknowledges his strength but raises practical dangers, including falling during the flight and being recaptured if they are attacked. She asks him to bring Rama instead. Her response includes both practical reasoning and commitments specific to the narrative.',lesson:'Hope and careful judgment can coexist. You can appreciate someone’s offer of help while asking whether the plan accounts for your needs and risks.',practice:'Before accepting an urgent plan, ask what could go wrong, who would help, and what alternative is available.',nuance:'The passage also speaks about marital fidelity and Rama’s reputation. Those narrative values should not be turned into a rule that a person in danger must refuse available rescue.',ref:'Valmiki Ramayana · Sundara Kanda 5.37',url:'https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/sundara/sarga37/sundara_37_prose.htm'},
{id:'lakshmana',name:'Lakshmana',sa:'लक्ष्मण',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Turn loyalty into practical help',role:'Rama’s brother and companion during exile.',story:'Lakshmana asks to accompany Rama and Sita into the forest. When Rama raises concern for the mothers left behind, Lakshmana answers that concern and describes concrete service: leading the way and gathering food. Rama then agrees to his company.',lesson:'Support becomes meaningful when it names what you will do and considers the other people affected by your promise.',practice:'Instead of saying only “I’m here for you,” offer one specific task you can reliably complete and check that it is actually wanted.',nuance:'Lakshmana’s commitment belongs to the epic’s family and devotional setting. A modern analogy should still allow consent, rest and limits rather than demanding unlimited self-sacrifice.',ref:'Valmiki Ramayana · Ayodhya Kanda 2.31',url:'https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/ayodhya/sarga31/ayodhya_31_prose.htm'},
{id:'vibhishana',name:'Vibhishana',sa:'विभीषण',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Care sometimes requires disagreement',role:'Ravana’s brother; offers unwelcome counsel in Lanka’s assembly.',story:'Vibhishana urges Ravana to return Sita before war brings disaster to Lanka. When Prahasta dismisses the danger, Vibhishana repeats his warning and asks the assembly to save the king by correcting his course.',lesson:'Loyalty to a person need not mean agreement with a harmful decision. Honest counsel can be an act of care even when it is unwelcome.',practice:'When a friend proposes something harmful, state the concern plainly, explain who could be affected, and suggest a responsible alternative.',nuance:'This scene concerns counsel within a royal assembly. It does not prove that every disagreement is righteous; examine your reasons and listen to evidence too.',ref:'Valmiki Ramayana · Yuddha Kanda 6.14',url:'https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/yuddha/sarga14/yuddha_14_prose.htm'},
{id:'jatayu',name:'Jatayu',sa:'जटायुः',epic:'Ramayana',trait:'Do not look away from another’s distress',role:'An aged bird-king who confronts Ravana during Sita’s abduction.',story:'Hearing Sita’s cries, Jatayu challenges Ravana and demands her release. He appeals to a ruler’s obligation to protect others and acknowledges the unequal contest between his own age and Ravana’s weapons.',lesson:'Another person’s distress asks something of a witness. Help can begin with recognizing the harm and taking a responsible step instead of treating it as someone else’s problem.',practice:'If you witness harassment, seek appropriate help, support the affected person, and choose an intervention that accounts for everyone’s safety.',nuance:'Epic courage is not an instruction to enter a dangerous confrontation alone. Calling for help or acting with others can be a more responsible modern response.',ref:'Valmiki Ramayana · Aranya Kanda 3.50',url:'https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/aranya/sarga50/aranya_50_prose.htm'}
);
const FORMATIONS=[
{id:'chakra',name:'Chakra-vyuha',sa:'चक्रव्यूह',shape:'Circular array',source:'Drona Parva, Ganguli §XXXIII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m07/m07033.htm',text:'Yudhishthira asks Abhimanyu to break Drona’s circular array. Abhimanyu says his father taught him entry but that he cannot get out if danger overtakes him. The others promise to follow.',points:['Entry knowledge is explicitly distinguished from the ability to return.','The plan depends on supporting warriors following the opening.','This passage says he learned from his father; it does not tell the popular “learning in the womb” story.'],lesson:'Modern analogy: entering a difficult project is only one part of planning. Clarify support, contingencies and an exit before committing.',labels:['Outer positions','Inner positions','Entry route']},
{id:'garuda',name:'Garuda-vyuha',sa:'गरुड़व्यूह',shape:'Eagle-shaped array',source:'Bhishma Parva, Ganguli §LVI',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m06/m06056.htm',text:'Bhishma arranges the Kaurava force in a Garuda array. The account places Bhishma at its beak, Drona and Kritavarman at its eyes, and Duryodhana on its back.',points:['The text assigns named leaders to different parts of the array.','Its bird metaphor organizes the description of positions.','The schematic shows relationships, not troop counts, distances or a proven tactical mechanism.'],lesson:'Modern analogy: define who leads, who supports, and who remains responsible for coordination.',labels:['Bhishma · beak','Drona / Kritavarman · eyes','Duryodhana · back']},
{id:'krauncha',name:'Krauncha-vyuha',sa:'क्रौञ्चव्यूह',shape:'Crane-shaped array',source:'Drona Parva, Ganguli §VII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m07/m07007.htm',text:'At the beginning of Drona’s command, the Pandava force is described as a Krauncha array arranged by Yudhishthira. Krishna and Arjuna stand at its head.',points:['The formation is described using the image of a crane.','Krishna and Arjuna are named at the head of the Pandava array.','A name and a few positions do not establish an exact geometric reconstruction.'],lesson:'Modern analogy: align the leading role with the skills most needed for the immediate task.',labels:['Krishna & Arjuna','Supporting positions','Body of the array']},
{id:'sakata',name:'Shakata-vyuha',sa:'शकटव्यूह',shape:'Cart-shaped array',source:'Drona Parva, Ganguli §VII',url:'https://sacred-texts.com/hin/m07/m07007.htm',text:'The same passage describes Drona arranging the Kaurava army in the form of a shakata, or vehicle, opposite the Pandavas’ crane array.',points:['Drona is identified as the arranger of this force.','“Vehicle” or “cart” describes its named form.','Claims about precise wheel rotations, dimensions or guaranteed advantages are not supplied here.'],lesson:'Modern analogy: a structure should serve its purpose. A memorable label alone does not explain how a plan works.',labels:['Front positions','Body of the array','Supporting positions']}
];
